# A village that runs itself, one week at a time.
#
# There is no `fact` clause here: the starting cast lives in story.graphfacts,
# one edge per line, and every capitalised name in that file becomes a node id.
# A bare number there is a YEAR (`Aldric born in 962`), which the loader turns
# into the week node of that year - the same node `week(x)` names in a clause.
#
# Three things about this language shape every rule below.
#
# 1. A WHEN edge the THEN clause does not RESTATE is REMOVED. That is the whole
#    mechanism - there is no delete keyword. So "keep being alive" has to be
#    written out in the THEN of every rule that is not trying to kill someone,
#    and forgetting it is how a rule quietly unmakes the world it matched.
#
# 2. A variable that appears only in the THEN is a NEW node. It is written with a
#    trailing quote - C' - purely so a reader can see it is being born rather
#    than bound; the compiler already treats any THEN-only variable that way.
#    A `'` variable may also be BOUND to a value: `D' = week(now() + 5)` makes D'
#    the node for that week, so a date is an edge later rules can read.
#
# 3. A match is INJECTIVE: two variables never bind the same node. `A likes B,
#    B likes A` is two people; and two people smitten in the same week share one
#    week node, so a rule that matches `A smitten on D, B smitten on E` would
#    silently never fire for them. Match a date only where it is read or removed.
#
# Lowercase words are literal. `A is woman` is the edge type "? is woman";
# `A is Woman` would make Woman a second VARIABLE, which is not what you want.
#
# Time. One tick is one week. `now()` is the absolute week; `week(x)` names the
# node for week x; `passed(D)`, `inTime(D)`, `weeksLeft(D, n)` compare a date
# node to now; `olderThan(D, years)` is how an age is tested; `roll(lo, hi)` is
# a seeded span so a promise can be "some months out" and still reproducible.

let Person is alive.
let Person is dead.
let Person is man.
let Person is woman.
let Person born in Week.

# There is no negation in this language, so "not married", "old enough", "not
# already in love" cannot be tested - they have to be edges that exist, and that
# a rule CONSUMES when the condition stops being true.
let Person is young.
let Person is an infant.
let Person is of age.
let Person is grown.
let Person is unmarried.
let Person is unattached.
let Person is fertile.

# Affection. `smitten on` is the week a liking formed: a liking must be a
# fortnight old before it can be returned, which is the window the kin rules
# use to kill one that should never have formed.
let Person0 likes Person1.
let Person smitten on Week.

let Person0 is wife of Person1.
let Person0 is husband of Person1.
let Person0 is child of Person1.
let Person0 is mother of Person1.
let Person0 is father of Person1.

let Person0 hates Person1.
let Person0 killed Person1.
let Person cries for vengeance.

# Friendship. `esteems` is one-sided and dated, the way a liking is: returned
# after a fortnight it becomes a friendship on both sides. A friend is who a
# plotter recruits, who warns an intended victim, and who takes up a blood
# debt when there is no son to take it.
let Person0 esteems Person1.
let Person esteemed on Week.
let Person0 is friend of Person1.

# The blood price. A killing can be paid for instead of answered: the kin
# accept, or refuse and take it up themselves. A feud between houses can be
# ended the same way.
let Person0 owes for Person1.
let Person0 paid weregild to Person1.

# Betrothal. `is engaged to` is consumed when a date is set; `is to wed` marks
# the couple between then and the wedding, and is what a rival plots against.
# `is promised to` is the extra mark an ARRANGED match carries: it is what an
# elopement breaks.
let Person0 is engaged to Person1.
let Person0 is to wed Person1.
let Person0 is promised to Person1.
let Place is a church in Place1.
let Person wed in Place.
let Person weds on Week.
let Person0 was jilted by Person1.

# Children take forty weeks.
let Person is with child.
let Person0 carries child of Person1.
let Person is due on Week.

# A plot has a target, a deadline, and a week before which it is not ripe. One
# plot at a time: `keeps the peace` is consumed when a plot is hatched and comes
# back when it is resolved, so a grievance does not hatch a fresh plot every
# week. `plots alone` is consumed by the one accomplice a plot may gain. An
# accomplice may turn.
let Person keeps the peace.
let Person0 plots against Person1.
let Person plots before Week.
let Person plots since Week.
let Person plots alone.
let Person0 conspires with Person1.
let Person0 betrayed Person1.
let Person0 was warned by Person1.
let Person was poisoned.
let Person was stabbed.
let Person was drowned.

# Grief, as a consumable, and a mourning period as a date.
let Person0 mourns Person1.

# What someone KNOWS. `blames` is not an opinion: it is the knowledge that
# this man did that killing, and it is what every rule of vengeance consumes.
# Nobody avenges a death they never learned of, which is why a killing with
# no widow, no accomplice and no eavesdropper goes unanswered.
let Person0 blames Person1.
let Person0 blackmails Person1.
let Person widowed on Week.

# The realm. A king, his queen, an heir, and the houses sworn to him. A crown
# with no king on it is an edge - `wants a king` - which the heir consumes by
# taking it and a pretender consumes by seizing it; they compete for the same
# edge, so the claim pass decides, and the heir's rule is an always-rule while
# a pretender's is a chance.
let Person is king of Realm.
let Person is queen of Realm.
let Person is heir to Realm.
let House swears to Realm.
let Realm wants a king.
let Realm wants an heir.
let Person claims Realm.
let Person0 backs Person1.

# Walls are thin. Someone who overhears a plot may warn its victim, denounce a
# traitor to the crown, or say nothing; and the plotter may learn they were
# heard.
let Person0 overheard Person1.
let Person0 denounced Person1.
let Person is outlawed.
let Person was of Place.

# Houses. A house has a head, who arranges matches; a broken match sets houses
# feuding, and a feud puts every man of one house against every soul of the
# other. Peace is an edge because a feud cannot be tested for absence.
let Person is of house House.
let Person is head of House.
let House0 is at peace with House1.
let House0 feuds with House1.

# Guilt, as a consumable. `A killed C` is permanent - it is what vengeance
# and the blood price read - so it cannot be what sends a man to the priest,
# or he would confess the same killing every week for the rest of his life.
# This is laid on a killer by the rule that kills and taken off him by the
# confession, and it names the VICTIM: a man who killed twice has two of them
# and confesses twice, and - the reason it is not a one-place edge - two
# killings cannot both spend the same one in the same week.
let Person0 unshriven for Person1.

# The church. A priest serves one, and what he is for is that a killing has
# somewhere to go besides the grave: a man confesses, takes a penance, and is
# absolved - or the priest carries what he was told to his own grave, or does
# not. `holds the seal on` is deliberately NOT `blames`: it is knowledge that
# cannot travel, until a rule makes it travel.
let Person serves Place.
let Person0 confessed to Person1.
let Person0 holds the seal on Person1.
let Person is under penance.
let Person penance until Week.
let Person was absolved.
let Person lies unhallowed.

# Suspicion is not knowledge. `blames` is only ever set by a rule that had the
# killing in front of it; `suspects` is what a grieving man does with a feud
# and no evidence, and `accuses` is saying it out loud. An accusation is
# answered whether or not it was true, which is the point of keeping the two
# apart.
let Person0 suspects Person1.
let Person0 accuses Person1.

# Silver. A bad season puts a house in want, its head borrows from a
# neighbour, and a debt that comes due unpaid ruins him. A ruined man marries
# his daughter cheaply or goes after the man holding the note.
let House is in want.
let Person0 owes silver to Person1.
let Person silver due on Week.
let Person is ruined.

# The village everyone lives in. A rule's WHEN edges must all lie on ONE path,
# and "every man against every woman" has nothing joining A to B - it is a
# cross join, which the matcher cannot express. Routing both through a shared
# place makes the pattern connected, and bounds the quadratic.
let Person lives in Place.


# --- sets --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A named conjunction, expanded at its call site with its parameters renamed to
# the caller's arguments. Restating is not optional here, so naming the group
# means writing it once and using it twice, and the two copies cannot drift.

set living(A...) = A is alive.
set living-man(A) = A is alive, A is man.
set living-woman(A) = A is alive, A is woman.
set together(A, B) = A lives in V, B lives in V.

set married(A, B) = A is wife of B, B is husband of A.
set engaged(A, B) = A is engaged to B, B is engaged to A.
set betrothed(A, B) = A is to wed B, B is to wed A.
set promised(A, B) = A is promised to B, B is promised to A.
set courting(A, B) = A likes B, B likes A.
set friends(A, B) = A is friend of B, B is friend of A.
set grown(A...) = A is grown.
set single(A...) = A is unmarried.
set free(A...) = A is unattached.


# --- attraction --------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One heart at a time: `unattached` is consumed when a liking forms and comes
# back when it dies, so nobody courts two people at once and a liking has one
# date. These run once per PAIR of grown villagers every week - twenty people
# is nearly two hundred pairs - so the chance is per pair, and small.

rule he-notices-her maybe 1 of 60:
when together(A, B), living-man(A), living-woman(B), grown(A, B), free(A)
then together(A, B), living-man(A), living-woman(B), grown(A, B),
     A likes B, A smitten on D' (D' = week(now() + 2)).

rule she-notices-him maybe 1 of 60:
when together(A, B), living-woman(A), living-man(B), grown(A, B), free(A)
then together(A, B), living-woman(A), living-man(B), grown(A, B),
     A likes B, A smitten on D' (D' = week(now() + 2)).

# A fortnight on, if the other is free, it may be returned.

rule the-feeling-is-returned maybe 2:
when A likes B, A smitten on D (passed(D)), free(B), living(A, B)
then A likes B, A smitten on D, living(A, B), B likes A, B smitten on E' (E' = week(now())).

# An unreturned liking does not last forever.

rule the-feeling-fades maybe 1 of 26:
when A likes B, A smitten on D, living(A)
then living(A), A is unattached.

# --- blood -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A liking for one's own parent, child or sibling dies in the week it forms,
# before it can be returned. These fire always; the fortnight in `smitten on`
# is what guarantees they get there first.

rule not-her-own-father:
when A likes B, A smitten on D, A is child of B
then A is child of B, A is unattached.

rule not-his-own-child:
when A likes B, A smitten on D, B is child of A
then B is child of A, A is unattached.

rule not-a-sibling:
when A likes B, A smitten on D, A is child of P, B is child of P
then A is child of P, B is child of P, A is unattached.


# --- enmity ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Attraction forms freely, and hatred destroys it: while A hates B no liking
# between them survives, which is the same as "if they do not hate each other"
# from the outside and needs no absence check.

rule strangers-take-against-each-other maybe 1 of 800:
when together(A, B), living(A, B), grown(A, B)
then together(A, B), living(A, B), grown(A, B), A hates B.

rule hatred-poisons-affection:
when A hates B, A likes B, A smitten on D
then A hates B, A is unattached.

rule the-suit-is-rebuffed maybe 3:
when A hates B, B likes A, B smitten on D
then A hates B, B is unattached.

rule hatred-fades maybe 1 of 20:
when A hates B, living(A), A keeps the peace
then living(A), A keeps the peace.


# --- friendship ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The same shape as attraction, and for the same reasons: one-sided first,
# dated so that a rule which ACTS on it can require a fortnight's standing,
# and consumed by whatever resolves it. Friendship is not exclusive - a person
# may have several friends - so nothing like `unattached` gates it; the rate
# is per pair per week and small.

rule two-take-to-each-other maybe 1 of 500:
when together(A, B), living(A, B), grown(A, B)
then together(A, B), living(A, B), grown(A, B), A esteems B, A esteemed on D' (D' = week(now() + 2)).

rule the-esteem-is-returned maybe 4:
when A esteems B, A esteemed on D (passed(D)), living(A, B), grown(B)
then living(A, B), grown(B), friends(A, B).

rule the-esteem-comes-to-nothing maybe 1 of 20:
when A esteems B, A esteemed on D, living(A)
then living(A).

# Hatred ends a friendship; so does time, slowly.

rule hatred-ends-a-friendship:
when A hates B, friends(A, B)
then A hates B.

rule friendship-cools maybe 1 of 150:
when friends(A, B), living(A, B)
then living(A, B).

# A man who kills has no friend in the dead man's friends.

rule a-friend-turns-on-a-killer maybe 4:
when A killed C, X is friend of C, friends(A, X), living(A, X)
then A killed C, X is friend of C, living(A, X), X hates A.


# --- courtship to marriage ---------------------------------------------------
#
# `single` is consumed along with the courtship: getting engaged uses up being
# unmarried, which is the whole reason the edge exists. The smitten dates are
# swept up afterwards, one per person, because a rule matching both at once
# would never fire for a couple smitten in the same week.

rule they-get-engaged maybe 2:
when living-woman(A), living-man(B), courting(A, B), single(A, B)
then living-woman(A), living-man(B), engaged(A, B).

rule the-courtship-is-over:
when engaged(A, B), A smitten on D
then engaged(A, B).

# The wedding is weeks away, not days. Three rules, so some couples take
# longer than others; the date is a node both read back.

rule they-set-a-date maybe 3:
when engaged(A, B), living(A, B)
then living(A, B), betrothed(A, B), A weds on D' (D' = week(now() + 5)), B weds on D'.

rule they-take-their-time maybe 2:
when engaged(A, B), living(A, B)
then living(A, B), betrothed(A, B), A weds on D' (D' = week(now() + 10)), B weds on D'.

rule they-wait-for-a-better-season maybe 1:
when engaged(A, B), living(A, B)
then living(A, B), betrothed(A, B), A weds on D' (D' = week(now() + 20)), B weds on D'.

# On the day, if both still live. `availableAt` books the church for THIS week
# and refuses if another couple has it, so a second couple waits a week.

rule the-vows-are-said:
when betrothed(A, B), A weds on D (passed(D)), B weds on D, living-woman(A), living-man(B),
     A lives in V, C is a church in V (availableAt(C, now()))
then living-woman(A), living-man(B), A lives in V, C is a church in V,
     married(A, B), A wed in C, B wed in C.

# The bride joins her husband's house: a feud is between houses, and a man must
# not find his own wife on the other side of it. Matching is injective, so this
# only fires when the two houses differ - a couple of one house has nothing to
# do - and it is its own rule for the same reason: a wedding rule that matched
# both houses could never marry two cousins.

rule a-wife-joins-her-husbands-house:
when A is wife of B, A is of house H, B is of house K
then A is wife of B, B is of house K, A is of house K.

rule the-promise-is-spent:
when married(A, B), promised(A, B)
then married(A, B).

rule the-wedding-night:
when A is wife of B, A smitten on D
then A is wife of B.

rule the-wedding-morning:
when A is husband of B, A smitten on D
then A is husband of B.

# A betrothal to the dead is over, and the survivor is free again.

rule the-betrothed-mourns:
when A is dead, betrothed(A, B), A weds on D, B weds on D, living(B)
then A is dead, living(B), B is unmarried, B is unattached.

rule the-engagement-dies-with-him:
when A is dead, engaged(A, B), living(B)
then A is dead, living(B), B is unmarried, B is unattached.

rule the-promise-dies-with-him:
when A is dead, promised(A, B)
then A is dead.


# --- arranged matches --------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two heads of house in one village promise a woman of one house to a man of
# the other. The date is months or years out; the couple are betrothed AND promised, and neither is
# `single` any longer, so no love match can claim them - but a liking can
# still form, which is where elopements come from.

rule the-heads-make-a-match maybe 1 of 25:
when F is head of H, G is head of K, H is at peace with K, F lives in V, G lives in V, living(F, G),
     A is of house H, B is of house K, living-woman(A), living-man(B), grown(A, B), single(A, B)
then F is head of H, G is head of K, H is at peace with K, F lives in V, G lives in V, living(F, G),
     A is of house H, B is of house K, living-woman(A), living-man(B), grown(A, B),
     betrothed(A, B), promised(A, B), A weds on D' (D' = week(now() + roll(12, 110))), B weds on D'.

# A feud can be ended the same way: the heads make peace with a match.

rule the-heads-make-peace-with-a-match maybe 1 of 15:
when K feuds with H, F is head of H, G is head of K, F lives in V, G lives in V, living(F, G),
     A is of house H, B is of house K, living-woman(A), living-man(B), grown(A, B), single(A, B)
then F is head of H, G is head of K, H is at peace with K, K is at peace with H, F lives in V, G lives in V, living(F, G),
     A is of house H, B is of house K, living-woman(A), living-man(B), grown(A, B),
     betrothed(A, B), promised(A, B), A weds on D' (D' = week(now() + roll(8, 30))), B weds on D'.

# A match too near in blood is undone the week it is made. A widow carries her
# late husband's house, so the heads can offer her to a brother or a son of
# hers; the Church will not have it.

rule the-church-forbids-the-match:
when promised(A, B), betrothed(A, B), A weds on D, B weds on D, A is child of P, B is child of P
then A is child of P, B is child of P, A is unmarried, B is unmarried.

rule the-church-forbids-a-mother-and-son:
when promised(A, B), betrothed(A, B), A weds on D, B weds on D, B is child of A
then B is child of A, A is unmarried, B is unmarried.

rule the-church-forbids-a-father-and-daughter:
when promised(A, B), betrothed(A, B), A weds on D, B weds on D, A is child of B
then A is child of B, A is unmarried, B is unmarried.


# --- elopement ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A promised woman who has come to love another runs off with him while there
# is still a week before the wedding. The jilted man is free again, and his
# house takes against hers.

rule she-runs-off-with-her-lover maybe 3:
when promised(A, B), betrothed(A, B), A weds on D (inTime(D)), B weds on D, courting(A, X),
     living-woman(A), living-man(X), living(B), single(X), A is of house H, B is of house K
then living-woman(A), living-man(X), living(B), A is of house H, B is of house K,
     married(A, X), B is unmarried, B was jilted by A, K feuds with H.

rule he-runs-off-with-his-lover maybe 3:
when promised(A, B), betrothed(B, A), A weds on D (inTime(D)), B weds on D, courting(A, X),
     living-man(A), living-woman(X), living(B), single(X), A is of house H, B is of house K
then living-man(A), living-woman(X), living(B), A is of house H, B is of house K,
     married(X, A), B is unmarried, B was jilted by A, K feuds with H.

rule a-feud-breaks-the-peace:
when K feuds with H, K is at peace with H
then K feuds with H.

rule a-feud-breaks-the-peace-both-ways:
when K feuds with H, H is at peace with K
then K feuds with H.

# The jilted: vengeance on the lover, a grudge, or letting it go.

rule the-jilted-man-plots-against-the-lover maybe 2:
when B was jilted by A, A is wife of X, living(A, B, X), grown(B), B keeps the peace
then A is wife of X, living(A, B, X), grown(B), B hates X,
     B plots against X, B plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(6, 20))), B plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 4))), B plots alone.

rule the-jilted-woman-plots-against-the-lover maybe 2:
when B was jilted by A, A is husband of X, living(A, B, X), grown(B), B keeps the peace
then A is husband of X, living(A, B, X), grown(B), B hates X,
     B plots against X, B plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(6, 20))), B plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 4))), B plots alone.

rule the-jilted-nurse-a-grudge maybe 3:
when B was jilted by A, living(B)
then living(B), B hates A.

rule the-jilted-let-it-go maybe 1:
when B was jilted by A, living(B)
then living(B).

# --- the feud ----------------------------------------------------------------

rule the-feud-claims-a-target maybe 1 of 400:
when K feuds with H, B is of house K, Y is of house H, living-man(B), grown(B), living(Y), grown(Y), B keeps the peace
then K feuds with H, B is of house K, Y is of house H, living-man(B), grown(B), living(Y), grown(Y),
     B hates Y, B plots against Y, B plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(4, 16))), B plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 4))), B plots alone.

rule the-houses-take-the-blood-price maybe 1 of 25:
when K feuds with H, F is head of H, G is head of K, living(F, G)
then F is head of H, G is head of K, living(F, G), H is at peace with K, K is at peace with H, G paid weregild to F.

rule the-feud-burns-out maybe 1 of 60:
when K feuds with H, F is head of K
then F is head of K, K is at peace with H.


# --- the rival ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Someone who wants the bride, or the groom, plots against the other. A plot is
# three edges: the target, the deadline, and the week it is ripe - a plot takes
# weeks to come together, and a method fires only once it is ripe and while
# there is still time. All the methods consume the plot, so one plot is one
# attempt.
#
# Every rule that ACTS on a liking reads its date with `passed(W)`: a liking
# must be a fortnight old. That is the window the blood rules use to kill one
# for a parent, a child or a sibling, and without the guard a son could plot
# against his father for his mother's sake in the week before it was killed.

rule he-plots-against-the-groom maybe 3:
when living-man(C), grown(C), living-woman(A), living-man(B), C likes A, C smitten on W (passed(W)), A is to wed B, B weds on D (weeksLeft(D, 3)), C keeps the peace
then living-man(C), grown(C), living-woman(A), living-man(B), C likes A, C smitten on W, A is to wed B, B weds on D,
     C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 3))), C plots alone.

rule she-plots-against-the-bride maybe 3:
when living-woman(C), grown(C), living-man(A), living-woman(B), C likes A, C smitten on W (passed(W)), A is to wed B, B weds on D (weeksLeft(D, 3)), C keeps the peace
then living-woman(C), grown(C), living-man(A), living-woman(B), C likes A, C smitten on W, A is to wed B, B weds on D,
     C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 3))), C plots alone.

# Coveting someone already married: the husband, or the wife, is in the way.

rule he-covets-another-mans-wife maybe 1 of 60:
when living-man(A), grown(A), A likes B, A smitten on W (passed(W)), living-woman(B), married(B, C), living(C), A keeps the peace
then living-man(A), grown(A), A likes B, A smitten on W, living-woman(B), married(B, C), living(C),
     A plots against C, A plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(6, 20))), A plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 4))), A plots alone.

rule she-covets-another-womans-husband maybe 1 of 90:
when living-woman(A), grown(A), A likes B, A smitten on W (passed(W)), living-man(B), married(C, B), living(C), A keeps the peace
then living-woman(A), grown(A), A likes B, A smitten on W, living-man(B), married(C, B), living(C),
     A plots against C, A plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(6, 20))), A plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 4))), A plots alone.

# --- accomplices and betrayal -------------------------------------------------
#
# A plotter may draw in someone with a reason of their own: one who hates the
# target, or one who loves the plotter. An accomplice may turn, and warn the
# target - who then hates the plotter, and may strike first.

rule the-plotter-finds-an-accomplice maybe 2:
when C plots against B, C plots before D (inTime(D)), C plots alone, X hates B, living(C, X, B), grown(X)
then C plots against B, C plots before D, X hates B, living(C, X, B), grown(X), X conspires with C.

rule a-friend-is-drawn-into-the-plot maybe 3:
when C plots against B, C plots before D (inTime(D)), C plots alone, X is friend of C, living(C, X, B), grown(X)
then C plots against B, C plots before D, X is friend of C, living(C, X, B), grown(X), X conspires with C.

rule a-lover-is-drawn-into-the-plot maybe 2:
when C plots against B, C plots before D (inTime(D)), C plots alone, X likes C, X smitten on W (passed(W)), living(C, X, B), grown(X)
then C plots against B, C plots before D, X likes C, X smitten on W, living(C, X, B), grown(X), X conspires with C.

# The accomplice who is the intended victim's friend warns him: one friendship
# betrays another. `keeps the peace` goes back to the plotter here, as it does
# wherever a plot ends - without it a plotter betrayed once could never plot
# again.

rule a-friend-warns-his-friend maybe 6:
when X conspires with C, C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E, X is friend of B, living(X, C, B)
then living(X, C, B), X is friend of B, C keeps the peace, X betrayed C, B was warned by X, B hates C.

rule the-accomplice-turns maybe 1 of 5:
when X conspires with C, C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E, living(X, C, B)
then living(X, C, B), C keeps the peace, X betrayed C, B was warned by X, B hates C.

rule the-accomplice-loses-heart maybe 1 of 10:
when X conspires with C, living(X)
then living(X).

rule the-betrayed-man-learns-of-it maybe 3:
when X betrayed C, living(C, X)
then living(C, X), C hates X.

rule a-betrayed-friendship-is-over:
when C hates X, friends(C, X)
then C hates X.

rule the-betrayal-stays-secret maybe 1:
when X betrayed C, living(C)
then living(C).

rule the-warned-strike-first maybe 3:
when B was warned by X, B hates C, living(B, C), grown(B), B keeps the peace
then living(B, C), grown(B), B hates C,
     B plots against C, B plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(4, 12))), B plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 3))), B plots alone.

rule the-warned-keep-their-guard maybe 2:
when B was warned by X, living(B)
then living(B).

# --- thin walls ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A plot is spoken aloud somewhere, and the village is small. The listener
# does not consume the plot, so the same plot can be overheard by several
# people; the insert is a duplicate after the first, which costs nothing.

rule a-thin-wall maybe 1 of 90:
when C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E, A lives in V, C lives in V, living(A, C), grown(A)
then C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E, A lives in V, C lives in V, living(A, C), grown(A), A overheard C.

rule the-eavesdropper-warns-the-victim maybe 3:
when A overheard C, C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E, A is friend of B, living(A, B, C)
then living(A, B, C), A is friend of B, C keeps the peace, B was warned by A, B hates C.

rule the-eavesdropper-holds-his-tongue maybe 2:
when A overheard C, living(A)
then living(A).

rule the-plotter-learns-he-was-heard maybe 1 of 6:
when A overheard C, C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E, living(A, C)
then C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E, living(A, C), C hates A.


# --- the realm ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The court runs on the same machinery as the village: a pretender plots with
# the plot rules, a denunciation is answered with outlawry, and a king who is
# murdered cries for vengeance like any other man.

rule the-king-takes-a-queen:
when K is king of R, K is husband of A, A is wife of K, living(K, A)
then K is king of R, K is husband of A, A is wife of K, living(K, A), A is queen of R.

rule the-heir-is-named maybe 5:
when R wants an heir, K is king of R, A is child of K, living-man(A), grown(A), living(K)
then K is king of R, A is child of K, living-man(A), grown(A), living(K), A is heir to R.

# The crown's authority over a house IS that house's oath, so a house has to
# be able to swear again: a king's house swears to nobody while he reigns, and
# without this it stayed outside the king's peace forever after he died - and
# a former royal house could not be outlawed however openly it plotted.

rule the-crown-is-vacant:
when K is dead, K is king of R, K is of house H
then K is dead, K is of house H, R wants a king, H swears to R.

rule the-heir-takes-the-throne:
when R wants a king, A is heir to R, living(A)
then living(A), A is king of R, R wants an heir.

rule a-sworn-house-seizes-the-throne maybe 3:
when R wants a king, H swears to R, F is head of H, living-man(F), grown(F)
then H swears to R, F is head of H, living-man(F), grown(F), F is king of R, R wants an heir.

rule the-pretender-seizes-the-throne maybe 5:
when R wants a king, F claims R, living(F)
then living(F), F is king of R, R wants an heir.

# A head of a sworn house who wants the crown. The plot is an ordinary plot:
# the methods, the accomplices and the eavesdroppers all apply to it.

rule a-claimant-plots-against-the-king maybe 1 of 15:
when K is king of R, H swears to R, F is head of H, living-man(F), grown(F), living(K), F keeps the peace
then K is king of R, H swears to R, F is head of H, living-man(F), grown(F), living(K), F claims R, F hates K,
     F plots against K, F plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(6, 30))), F plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(2, 6))), F plots alone.

rule the-kings-house-swears-to-no-one:
when K is king of R, K is of house H, H swears to R
then K is king of R, K is of house H.

# Declaring for a pretender does NOT dissolve the oath - and that matters,
# because the crown's authority over a man IS his house's oath. While backing
# consumed it, every house in a realm had declared for somebody within a year
# and the king could outlaw nobody: 724 denunciations, not one outlawry.

# Once per house per pretender, so the rate is per (claimant x house) per
# week and has to be small: at 1 in 10 a year of two hundred thousand people
# produced a million declarations, one from every house for every claimant.
rule a-house-backs-the-pretender maybe 1 of 300:
when F claims R, G is head of H, H swears to R, living(F, G), grown(G)
then F claims R, G is head of H, H swears to R, living(F, G), grown(G), G backs F.

rule the-claim-is-abandoned maybe 1 of 40:
when F claims R, living(F)
then living(F).

rule a-claim-dies-with-the-claimant:
when F is dead, F claims R
then F is dead.

rule the-backers-fall-away:
when F is dead, G backs F
then F is dead.

rule the-queen-is-no-more:
when A is dead, A is queen of R
then A is dead.

rule an-heir-who-does-not-live:
when A is dead, A is heir to R
then A is dead.

# Denunciation. A plot against the king overheard is a plot the crown hears
# about, and the crown answers by outlawry: the traitor loses the village he
# lived in, and may be hunted down or pardoned.

rule the-eavesdropper-denounces-a-traitor maybe 4:
when A overheard C, C plots against K, C plots before D, C plots since E, K is king of R, living(A, C, K)
then K is king of R, living(A, C, K), A denounced C, C keeps the peace.

rule the-crown-outlaws-the-traitor maybe 5:
when A denounced C, C is of house H, H swears to R, K is king of R, living(C, K), C lives in V
then C is of house H, H swears to R, K is king of R, living(C, K), C is outlawed, C was of V.

rule the-denunciation-is-ignored maybe 1:
when A denounced C, living(A)
then living(A).

rule an-outlaw-is-hunted-down maybe 1 of 10:
when C is outlawed, C was of V, living(C)
then C is dead, C was of V.

rule the-outlaw-is-pardoned maybe 1 of 50:
when C is outlawed, C was of V, C is of house H, H swears to R, K is king of R, living(C)
then C is of house H, H swears to R, K is king of R, living(C), C lives in V.

rule an-outlaw-in-his-grave:
when C is dead, C is outlawed, C was of V
then C is dead.


# --- the deed -----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The death is SCHEDULED: the victim goes on living until the poison takes, so a
# plotter who dies first leaves no one in limbo, and `is alive` leaves the week
# `is dead` lands. Every method fits inside the deadline - a slow poison needs
# two weeks in hand, a knife one.

rule poison maybe 1:
when C plots against B, C plots before D (weeksLeft(D, 2)), C plots since E (passed(E)), living(B, C)
then living(B, C), C keeps the peace,
     B is dead (occurAt(now() + 2)), C killed B (occurAt(now() + 2)), B was poisoned (occurAt(now() + 2)),
     C unshriven for B (occurAt(now() + 2)), B cries for vengeance (occurAt(now() + 2)).

rule a-knife-in-the-dark maybe 1:
when C plots against B, C plots before D (weeksLeft(D, 1)), C plots since E (passed(E)), living(B, C)
then living(B, C), C keeps the peace,
     B is dead (occurAt(now() + 1)), C killed B (occurAt(now() + 1)), B was stabbed (occurAt(now() + 1)),
     C unshriven for B (occurAt(now() + 1)), B cries for vengeance (occurAt(now() + 1)).

rule the-river maybe 1:
when C plots against B, C plots before D (weeksLeft(D, 1)), C plots since E (passed(E)), living(B, C)
then living(B, C), C keeps the peace,
     B is dead (occurAt(now() + 1)), C killed B (occurAt(now() + 1)), B was drowned (occurAt(now() + 1)),
     C unshriven for B (occurAt(now() + 1)), B cries for vengeance (occurAt(now() + 1)).

rule the-plot-comes-to-nothing maybe 2:
when C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E (passed(E)), living(C)
then living(C), C keeps the peace (occurAt(now() + roll(8, 30))).

# A plot still pending when its day arrives is over: nothing to stop any more.

rule the-moment-passes:
when C plots against B, C plots before D (passed(D)), C plots since E, living(C)
then living(C), C keeps the peace.

rule the-target-dies-anyway:
when B is dead, C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E, living(C)
then B is dead, living(C), C keeps the peace.

# Once a plot is resolved, the mark that it had no accomplice is meaningless.

rule the-plot-is-over:
when C plots alone, C keeps the peace
then C keeps the peace.

rule the-plotter-dies-first:
when C is dead, C plots against B, C plots before D, C plots since E
then C is dead.

rule the-accomplice-shares-the-guilt:
when X conspires with C, C killed B, B cries for vengeance
then C killed B, B cries for vengeance, X killed B, X unshriven for B.

# `is dead` arrives on a schedule; `is alive` leaves the moment they meet.

rule the-dead-are-dead:
when A is alive, A is dead
then A is dead.

# --- vengeance ---------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A killing cries out once: whoever answers - a son, the widow, a man of the
# house - consumes the cry, so one murder gets one avenger, and that avenger's
# killing cries out in turn. That is how a feud goes on without a feud edge.

rule blood-for-blood maybe 1 of 12:
when C cries for vengeance, A killed C, X blames A, X is child of C, living-man(X), grown(X), living(A), X keeps the peace
then A killed C, X is child of C, living-man(X), grown(X), living(A), X hates A,
     X plots against A, X plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(8, 30))), X plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(2, 6))), X plots alone.

rule a-father-avenges-his-son maybe 1 of 12:
when C cries for vengeance, A killed C, X blames A, C is child of X, living-man(X), grown(X), living(A), X keeps the peace
then A killed C, C is child of X, living-man(X), grown(X), living(A), X hates A,
     X plots against A, X plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(8, 30))), X plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(2, 6))), X plots alone.

rule the-widow-swears-vengeance maybe 1 of 15:
when C cries for vengeance, A killed C, B blames A, living(A, B), grown(B), B keeps the peace
then A killed C, living(A, B), grown(B), B hates A,
     B plots against A, B plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(8, 30))), B plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(2, 6))), B plots alone.

rule a-kinsman-takes-up-the-blood-debt maybe 1 of 40:
when C cries for vengeance, A killed C, X blames A, C is of house H, X is of house H, living-man(X), grown(X), living(A), X keeps the peace
then A killed C, C is of house H, X is of house H, living-man(X), grown(X), living(A), X hates A,
     X plots against A, X plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(8, 30))), X plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(2, 6))), X plots alone.

rule a-friend-avenges-a-friend maybe 1 of 15:
when C cries for vengeance, A killed C, X blames A, X is friend of C, living-man(X), grown(X), living(A), X keeps the peace
then A killed C, living-man(X), grown(X), living(A), X hates A,
     X plots against A, X plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(8, 30))), X plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(2, 6))), X plots alone.

# Or the kin name a price instead. A debt named is a debt that can be paid, or
# refused - and a refusal is a plot like any other.

rule the-kin-name-a-price maybe 1 of 10:
when C cries for vengeance, A killed C, X blames A, X is child of C, living(A, X), grown(X)
then A killed C, X is child of C, living(A, X), grown(X), A owes for C.

rule the-blood-price-is-paid maybe 3:
when A owes for C, X is child of C, living(A, X)
then X is child of C, living(A, X), A paid weregild to X.

rule the-price-is-refused maybe 1:
when A owes for C, X is child of C, living(A, X), grown(X), X keeps the peace
then X is child of C, living(A, X), grown(X), X hates A,
     X plots against A, X plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(8, 30))), X plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(2, 6))), X plots alone.

rule the-debt-dies-with-the-debtor:
when A is dead, A owes for C
then A is dead.

rule the-killing-goes-unavenged maybe 1 of 12:
when C cries for vengeance, C is dead
then C is dead.

# --- how a killing becomes known ----------------------------------------------
#
# Four ways: the widow is told, an accomplice was there, someone overheard the
# plot beforehand, or word travels between friends and kin. Each of these only
# ADDS knowledge, so a second telling is a duplicate and costs nothing.

rule the-accomplice-knew-all-along:
when X conspires with C, C killed B, living(X)
then X conspires with C, C killed B, living(X), X blames C.

rule the-eavesdropper-knew-the-plot:
when A overheard C, C killed B, living(A)
then A overheard C, C killed B, living(A), A blames C.

rule word-gets-around maybe 2:
when A blames C, friends(A, X), living(A, X)
then A blames C, friends(A, X), living(A, X), X blames C.

rule the-kin-are-told maybe 3:
when A blames C, C killed B, X is child of B, living(A, X)
then A blames C, C killed B, X is child of B, living(A, X), X blames C.

rule what-is-known-dies-with-the-knower:
when A is dead, A blames B
then A is dead.

# Knowing who did it is leverage as well as grief.

rule the-witness-asks-for-silence maybe 1 of 40:
when A blames C, C killed B, living(A, C), grown(A)
then C killed B, living(A, C), grown(A), A blackmails C.

rule the-blackmail-is-paid maybe 2:
when A blackmails C, living(A, C)
then living(A, C), C paid weregild to A.

rule the-blackmailer-is-silenced maybe 2:
when A blackmails C, living(A, C), grown(C), C keeps the peace
then living(A, C), grown(C), C hates A,
     C plots against A, C plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(4, 16))), C plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 4))), C plots alone.

rule blackmail-dies-with-the-blackmailer:
when A is dead, A blackmails B
then A is dead.

rule the-blackmailed-in-his-grave:
when B is dead, A blackmails B, living(A)
then B is dead, living(A).

rule the-widow-learns-who-killed-him maybe 5:
when B mourns C, A killed C, living(A, B)
then A killed C, living(A, B), B hates A, B blames A.

rule the-widow-forgoes-vengeance maybe 1:
when B blames A, living(B)
then living(B).

rule the-dead-blame-nobody:
when B is dead, B blames A
then B is dead.


# --- children ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Forty weeks. `fertile` is consumed at conception and comes back some months
# after the birth, so no woman bears twice in a year. The father is recorded at
# conception so a widow still bears his child.

rule she-conceives maybe 1 of 10:
when married(A, B), living(A, B), A is fertile
then married(A, B), living(A, B), A is with child, A carries child of B, A is due on D' (D' = week(now() + 40)).

# `or` expands this into two rules, one per gender; both match the same row and
# both consume `with child`, so the claim pass lets exactly one fire.

rule a-child-is-born:
when A is with child, A carries child of B, A is due on D (passed(D)), living(A), A lives in V, B is of house H
then living(A), A lives in V, B is of house H,
     C' is alive, C' is young, C' is an infant, C' is woman or C' is man, C' lives in V, C' is of house H,
     C' is child of A, C' is child of B, A is mother of C', B is father of C', C' born in W' (W' = week(now())),
     A is fertile (occurAt(now() + roll(30, 80))).

rule she-dies-in-childbed maybe 1 of 30:
when A is with child, A carries child of B, A is due on D (passed(D)), A is alive
then A is dead.

# Growing up is what makes someone eligible: at sixteen `young` is consumed,
# `grown`, `unmarried` and `unattached` appear, and only then do the attraction
# rules see them.

rule comes-of-age:
when A is young, A born in D (olderThan(D, 16)), living(A)
then A born in D, living(A), A is of age.

rule a-young-man-grown:
when A is of age, A is man
then A is man, A is grown, A is unmarried, A is unattached, A keeps the peace.

rule a-young-woman-grown:
when A is of age, A is woman
then A is woman, A is grown, A is unmarried, A is unattached, A is fertile, A keeps the peace.

rule her-childbearing-years-end:
when A is fertile, A born in D (olderThan(D, 45))
then A born in D.


# --- the confessional ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# A killing has three ends in this world: it is avenged, it is paid for, or it
# is confessed. The third is the only one that touches the killer rather than
# the corpse, and it is the only channel that can go either way - a priest who
# keeps the seal takes the knowledge out of the world, and a priest who breaks
# it puts a name in the widow's mouth.
#
# `holds the seal on` is deliberately not `blames`, so it does not travel:
# word-gets-around matches friends of someone who BLAMES, and the priest
# never does.

rule a-killer-confesses maybe 1 of 30:
when A unshriven for C, A killed C, A lives in V, K is a church in V, P serves K, living(A, P), grown(A)
then A killed C, A lives in V, K is a church in V, P serves K, living(A, P), grown(A),
     A confessed to P, P holds the seal on A.

rule the-priest-sets-a-penance maybe 4:
when A confessed to P, living(A, P)
then living(A, P), A is under penance, A penance until Q' (Q' = week(now() + roll(10, 60))).

# A man doing penance puts down whatever he was planning. It is the only thing
# in this world that ends a plot without a death or a betrayal.

rule a-penitent-gives-up-his-plot maybe 3:
when A is under penance, A plots against B, A plots before D, A plots since E, A plots alone, living(A)
then A is under penance, living(A), A keeps the peace.

rule the-penance-is-done:
when A is under penance, A penance until Q (passed(Q)), living(A)
then living(A), A was absolved.

# The priest's own decision, and it is a coin: he takes it to his grave, or the
# widow gets a name.

rule the-priest-keeps-the-seal maybe 1 of 60:
when P holds the seal on A, living(P)
then living(P).

rule the-priest-breaks-the-seal maybe 1 of 40:
when P holds the seal on A, A killed C, B mourns C, living(P, A, B), grown(B)
then A killed C, B mourns C, living(P, A, B), grown(B), B blames A, B hates A.

# A widow only exists if the dead man was married, and mourning fades, so on
# its own the rule above almost never found anyone to tell. A child is
# permanent.

rule the-priest-tells-the-son maybe 1 of 50:
when P holds the seal on A, A killed C, X is child of C, living(P, A, X), grown(X)
then A killed C, X is child of C, living(P, A, X), grown(X), X blames A, X hates A.

# A killer who never confessed is refused hallowed ground, which is the
# village's last word on him.

rule the-unshriven-are-refused-burial maybe 2:
when A unshriven for C, A is dead, A lives in V, K is a church in V, P serves K, living(P)
then A is dead, A lives in V, K is a church in V, P serves K, living(P), A lies unhallowed.

# A parish does not stay empty: a grown man of the village takes the living.

rule a-new-priest-is-ordained maybe 2:
when P is dead, P serves K, K is a church in V, X lives in V, living-man(X), grown(X)
then P is dead, K is a church in V, X lives in V, living-man(X), grown(X), X serves K.

rule the-seal-dies-with-the-priest:
when P is dead, P holds the seal on A
then P is dead.

rule the-penitent-in-his-grave:
when A is dead, A is under penance, A penance until Q
then A is dead.

rule a-confession-dies-with-the-confessor:
when A is dead, A confessed to P
then A is dead.

rule nothing-is-held-against-a-corpse:
when A is dead, P holds the seal on A, living(P)
then A is dead, living(P).

rule guilt-outlives-nobody:
when A is dead, A unshriven for C
then A is dead.

rule absolution-outlives-nobody:
when A is dead, A was absolved
then A is dead.


# --- suspicion ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Grief wants a name and a feud supplies one. Nothing here consults who
# actually did the killing, and that is deliberate: `suspects` and `accuses`
# are what a man believes, `blames` is what the world has shown him, and the
# point of keeping them apart is that an accusation can be wrong and still be
# answered.

rule suspicion-falls-on-the-other-house maybe 1 of 8:
when C cries for vengeance, C is of house H, K feuds with H, B is of house K, X is child of C,
     living(B, X), grown(X), living-man(B)
then C cries for vengeance, C is of house H, K feuds with H, B is of house K, X is child of C,
     living(B, X), grown(X), living-man(B), X suspects B.

rule a-widow-suspects-the-other-house maybe 1 of 10:
when X mourns C, C is of house H, K feuds with H, B is of house K, living(B, X), grown(X), living-man(B)
then X mourns C, C is of house H, K feuds with H, B is of house K, living(B, X), grown(X), living-man(B),
     X suspects B.

# And a son with nobody to blame looks around the village. There is no feud
# behind this one and no evidence in it either; it is the plainest way this
# world produces a wrong answer.

rule the-son-looks-for-someone-to-blame maybe 1 of 50:
when C cries for vengeance, X is child of C, C lives in V, B lives in V, B is of house K,
     living(B, X), grown(X), living-man(B)
then C cries for vengeance, X is child of C, C lives in V, B lives in V, B is of house K,
     living(B, X), grown(X), living-man(B), X suspects B.

rule the-suspicion-spreads maybe 2:
when X suspects B, friends(X, Y), living(X, Y, B)
then X suspects B, friends(X, Y), living(X, Y, B), Y suspects B.

rule the-suspicion-is-said-aloud maybe 1 of 5:
when X suspects B, living(X, B), grown(X)
then living(X, B), grown(X), X accuses B, X hates B.

rule the-suspicion-comes-to-nothing maybe 1 of 6:
when X suspects B, living(X)
then living(X).

# An accusation is answered whether or not it was true. Either the houses fall
# out over it, or the man named decides the only answer left to him is the one
# he was accused of.

rule the-houses-fall-out-over-an-accusation maybe 3:
when X accuses B, X is of house H, B is of house K, H is at peace with K, K is at peace with H,
     living(X, B)
then X accuses B, X is of house H, B is of house K, living(X, B), H feuds with K, K feuds with H.

rule the-accused-strikes-back maybe 1 of 4:
when X accuses B, living(B, X), grown(B), B keeps the peace
then X accuses B, living(B, X), grown(B), B hates X,
     B plots against X, B plots before D' (D' = week(now() + roll(6, 24))), B plots since E' (E' = week(now() + roll(1, 4))), B plots alone.

rule the-accuser-thinks-better-of-it maybe 1 of 8:
when X accuses B, living(X)
then living(X).

rule the-dead-suspect-nobody:
when X is dead, X suspects B
then X is dead.

rule nobody-suspects-a-corpse:
when B is dead, X suspects B, living(X)
then B is dead, living(X).

rule an-accusation-dies-with-the-accuser:
when X is dead, X accuses B
then X is dead.

rule the-accused-in-his-grave:
when B is dead, X accuses B, living(X)
then B is dead, living(X).


# --- silver ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A house has a bad season, its head borrows from a neighbour, and the note
# comes due. What ruin does is push a head into the two things he would not
# otherwise do: marry a daughter off cheap, or go after the man holding the
# note. It is the only pressure in this world that comes from outside the
# people in it.

rule a-hard-season maybe 1 of 90:
when F is head of H, living(F)
then F is head of H, living(F), H is in want.

rule the-head-borrows maybe 4:
when H is in want, F is head of H, G is head of K, F lives in V, G lives in V, living(F, G)
then F is head of H, G is head of K, F lives in V, G lives in V, living(F, G),
     F owes silver to G, F silver due on D' (D' = week(now() + roll(12, 40))).

rule the-house-comes-through maybe 1 of 20:
when H is in want, F is head of H, living(F)
then F is head of H, living(F).

rule the-debt-is-settled maybe 1 of 60:
when F owes silver to G, F silver due on D, living(F, G)
then living(F, G).

rule the-note-comes-due:
when F owes silver to G, F silver due on D (passed(D)), living(F, G)
then F owes silver to G, living(F, G), F is ruined.

# A ruined head marries his daughter to whoever will have her. It is the match
# rule with the bargaining taken out of it, which is why the dates are short.

rule a-ruined-house-marries-down maybe 3:
when F is ruined, F is head of H, A is of house H, living-woman(A), grown(A), single(A),
     G is head of K, B is of house K, living-man(B), grown(B), single(B), F lives in V, G lives in V
then F is ruined, F is head of H, A is of house H, living-woman(A), grown(A),
     G is head of K, B is of house K, living-man(B), grown(B), F lives in V, G lives in V,
     betrothed(A, B), promised(A, B), A weds on M' (M' = week(now() + roll(4, 20))), B weds on M'.

rule a-ruined-man-turns-on-his-creditor maybe 1 of 6:
when F is ruined, F owes silver to G, living(F, G), grown(F), F keeps the peace
then F is ruined, F owes silver to G, living(F, G), grown(F), F hates G,
     F plots against G, F plots before M' (M' = week(now() + roll(6, 26))), F plots since N' (N' = week(now() + roll(2, 5))), F plots alone.

rule the-house-recovers maybe 1 of 30:
when F is ruined, living(F)
then living(F).

rule silver-dies-with-the-debtor:
when F is dead, F owes silver to G, F silver due on D
then F is dead.

rule a-note-dies-with-the-lender:
when G is dead, F owes silver to G, F silver due on D, living(F)
then G is dead, living(F).

rule ruin-outlives-nobody:
when F is dead, F is ruined
then F is dead.

rule want-outlives-its-head:
when F is dead, F is head of H, H is in want
then F is dead, F is head of H.


# --- death -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The rules whose THEN deliberately drops `is alive`. `death-comes` is scaled by
# the driver with the population: a tenth of its chance below the cap - which
# at one in five hundred rounds to nothing - and rising with the excess above
# it, so the cap is the only thing it does. Natural death is by age: infants
# under five, the middle-aged past forty-five, and the old past sixty-five.

rule death-comes maybe 1 of 500:
when A is alive
then A is dead.

rule a-child-sickens maybe 1 of 1000:
when A is alive, A is an infant
then A is dead, A is an infant.

rule out-of-swaddling:
when A is an infant, A born in D (olderThan(D, 5))
then A born in D.

rule a-sudden-illness maybe 1 of 1000:
when A is alive, A born in D (olderThan(D, 45))
then A is dead, A born in D.

rule old-age maybe 1 of 100:
when A is alive, A born in D (olderThan(D, 65))
then A is dead, A born in D.

# Death does not dissolve a marriage by itself - `death-comes` never matched the
# marriage, so it could not remove it. These do the tidying, and fire always so
# no corpse stays married for a week longer than it has to. Widowhood gives
# `unmarried` back only once the mourning is done.

rule the-widow-mourns:
when A is dead, A is husband of B, B is wife of A
then A is dead, B mourns A, B widowed on D' (D' = week(now() + roll(8, 40))).

rule the-widower-mourns:
when A is dead, A is wife of B, B is husband of A
then A is dead, B mourns A, B widowed on D' (D' = week(now() + roll(8, 40))).

rule the-mourning-ends:
when B widowed on D (passed(D)), living(B)
then living(B), B is unmarried, B is unattached.

rule grief-fades maybe 1 of 30:
when B mourns C, living(B)
then living(B).

# A house whose head has died passes to a son, or failing that to any grown man
# of the house.

rule the-house-passes-to-a-son maybe 5:
when F is dead, F is head of H, X is child of F, living-man(X), grown(X)
then F is dead, X is child of F, living-man(X), grown(X), X is head of H.

rule the-house-passes-to-a-kinsman maybe 1:
when F is dead, F is head of H, X is of house H, living-man(X), grown(X)
then F is dead, X is of house H, living-man(X), grown(X), X is head of H.

# A dead man's affections, hatreds and plots stop being anyone's problem.

rule the-dead-stop-wanting:
when A is dead, A likes B, A smitten on D
then A is dead.

rule the-heart-lets-go-of-the-dead:
when A likes B, A smitten on D, B is dead, living(A)
then B is dead, living(A), A is unattached.

rule the-dead-stop-hating:
when A is dead, A hates B
then A is dead.

rule nobody-hates-a-corpse:
when B is dead, A hates B, living(A)
then B is dead, living(A).

rule grief-outlives-nobody:
when A is dead, A mourns B
then A is dead.

rule the-dead-are-not-widowed:
when A is dead, A widowed on D
then A is dead.

rule the-dead-bear-nothing:
when A is dead, A is with child, A carries child of B, A is due on D
then A is dead.

rule the-dead-have-no-friends:
when A is dead, A is friend of B
then A is dead.

rule nobody-befriends-a-corpse:
when B is dead, A is friend of B, living(A)
then B is dead, living(A).

rule the-dead-stop-esteeming:
when A is dead, A esteems B, A esteemed on D
then A is dead.

rule esteem-dies-with-the-esteemed:
when B is dead, A esteems B, A esteemed on D, living(A)
then B is dead, living(A).

rule the-dead-hear-nothing:
when A is dead, A overheard B
then A is dead.

rule nobody-overhears-a-corpse:
when B is dead, A overheard B, living(A)
then B is dead, living(A).

rule a-denunciation-dies-with-the-accuser:
when A is dead, A denounced B
then A is dead.

rule the-dead-back-nobody:
when A is dead, A backs B
then A is dead.

rule the-dead-conspire-with-nobody:
when X is dead, X conspires with C
then X is dead.
